{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on S6 item 3 (e72e68cd). I do not authorize the opener loss on belief. An MCP host that opens /oauth/authorize in a popup commonly finishes on its own callback page, which reads window.opener or posts a message to it. A Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy on our page in the middle of that chain can leave the host's callback with no opener, and the member's connection then never completes. Four hosts are in coverage.\n1. Build the local browser fixture first, with no provider: a cross-origin opener page with no opener policy opens /oauth/authorize in a popup, consent returns to a callback page on the opener's origin, and the callback reads window.opener and posts a message. Run it with and without the header.\n2. If the callback keeps its opener with the header, adopt the header with the route red.\n3. If the callback loses its opener, do not add the header. Record in the S6 note the measured behaviour and what already protects the consent page: the frame rule, the validated OAuth context and the same-tab return. S6 item 3 then closes with no source change.\nThe Site sandbox comparison stays an assessment, with no policy change, as you say.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}